# AGENTS.md

This file orients AI coding assistants (Claude, Cursor, Copilot, etc.) working
in this repository. It was generated once by `esb init` and is not updated
automatically as the project evolves — treat it as a map, not a spec.

## What this project is

`{{.ModuleName}}` is a Go event-sourcing service scaffolded by
[esb](https://github.com/ariefsam/esb) (Event Sourcing Boilerplate). Domain
state is derived by replaying events; a read model (projection) is kept in
sync separately for queries.

## Structure

- `domain/` — aggregates. Each aggregate has `Apply()` (mutate state from an
  event), `Replay()` (rebuild state from history), and `Exists()`.
- `eventstore/` — event store client. `local_store.go` is the embedded SQLite
  store; `client.go` is the HTTP client for a remote ESB server.
- `repository/` — adapters implementing `domain.EventRepository` over
  whichever event store is active (`local_adapter.go` / `eventstore_adapter.go`).
- `projection/` — read-model DB (GORM), migrations, and query helpers.
- `server/` — HTTP routes/handlers.
- `wire/` — manual dependency injection; `wire.go` builds the `App` and picks
  the event store implementation based on `EVENT_STORE_MODE`.
- `main.go`, `Makefile`, `.env.example` — entrypoint, build/run tasks, config.

## Codegen markers

Files contain `// esb:inject:<name>` comments (e.g. in `wire/wire.go`,
`server/routes.go`, `projection/db.go`). The `esb add` CLI inserts generated
code at these markers. When editing these files by hand, keep the markers
intact and add new code near them so future `esb add` runs don't conflict.

## Snapshots

Each `service/<aggregate>.go` auto-snapshots the aggregate every
`<Aggregate>SnapshotInterval` versions (default 100) so `load()` restores
from the latest snapshot plus only the events after it, instead of
replaying full history every time. The aggregate struct is JSON-marshaled
as-is to build the snapshot — see the comment on the aggregate struct in
`domain/<name>.go` before adding non-serializable fields.

## Distributed locks

`wire.App.Locker` (type `domain.Locker`) gives mutual exclusion across
replicas — `AcquireLock`/`ReleaseLock`/`RefreshLock`/`GetLock`, keyed by an
arbitrary string. Use it for leader election, serializing a scheduled job,
or any "only one instance should do X" need. Errors `domain.ErrLockBusy`
(someone else holds it) and `domain.ErrLockNotOwned` (your token no longer
matches, e.g. after expiry) are sentinel-checkable with `errors.Is`. Backed
by the same store as events (HTTP in esb-server mode, a local `locks`
SQLite table in embedded mode).

## Testing command methods

Use the `testkit` package + `eventstore.NewFakeStore()` (an in-memory
`EventRepository` with the same optimistic-concurrency/snapshot semantics as
the real stores) to test command methods Given-When-Then style, without a
database:

```go
testkit.Given(t, repo, domain.OrderAggregateName, "order-1").
    When(func(ctx context.Context) error { return svc.PlaceOrder(ctx, "order-1", 5000) }).
    Then(testkit.Event("OrderPlaced", map[string]any{"amount": int64(5000)}))
```

Each `esb add aggregate` generates a starter `service/<name>_scenario_test.go`
with a TODO placeholder — replace it once real events/command methods exist.

## Event store modes

Set via `EVENT_STORE_MODE` in `.env`:

- `embedded` (default) — local SQLite, no external service required.
- `esb-server` — remote HTTP event store; requires `ESB_URL` and a signing
  key from `make keygen`.

`make migrate-to-esb` moves events from embedded to a remote server.

## Extending this project

This project is meant to be extended using the `esb` CLI (`esb add
aggregate|event|handler ...`), not by hand-rolling the same boilerplate.

For whole common patterns, prefer a **recipe** over wiring components by hand —
each generates a complete, tested vertical slice in one atomic step:

- `esb add recipe crud <name> [field:type ...]` — entity CRUD with soft delete
  (Create/Update/Archive), read model, queries, handler, scenario tests.
- `esb add recipe ledger <name>` — append-only account (Open/Deposit/Withdraw/
  Freeze/Close) with a non-negative-balance invariant and a statement journal.
- `esb add recipe statemachine <name> --states ... --transitions ...` — a
  lifecycle aggregate with a transition table that rejects illegal moves.
- `esb add recipe saga <name>` — an orchestration saga: a two-step transfer
  through a Port interface that compensates (refunds) when the second leg fails.
- `esb add recipe outbox <name>` — a transactional outbox: an ingest worker
  (events → outbox table) + a publisher worker relaying them to the outside.
- `esb add upcaster <aggregate> <EventName>` — migrate stored event payloads
  from an older shape to the current one on read (schema evolution).
- `esb add idempotency` — a reusable guard (AlreadyProcessed / Once) so commands
  can be made safe to retry, keyed by an event's IdempotencyKey.

**Run `esb --help` and `esb <command> --help` for the authoritative,
up-to-date usage** — available commands and flags may have changed since
this file was generated. If `esb` isn't installed, run
`go install github.com/ariefsam/esb@latest`.
